%S A400233 16,79,224,463,784,1151,1504,1759,1808,1519,736,-721,-3056,-6497,
%T A400233 -11296,-17729,-26096,-36721,-49952,-66161,-85744,-109121,-136736,
%U A400233 -169057,-206576,-249809,-299296,-355601,-419312,-491041,-571424,-661121,-760816,-871217,-993056
%N A400233 a(n) is the number of boundary lattice points minus the number of interior lattice points of the 4-dimensional cube [0,n]^4.
%C A400233 The 4-dimensional cube [0,n]^4 has (n-1)^4 interior lattice points (those with every coordinate strictly between 0 and n) and (n+1)^4 - (n-1)^4 boundary lattice points. a(n) is the boundary count minus the interior count.
%C A400233 a(n) reaches a maximum of 1808 at n = 9 and is negative for n >= 12, since theinterior count grows like n^4 while the boundary count grows like n^3.
%C A400233 The corresponding difference for an n X n X n cube is A400232; more generally, for a d-dimensional cube the difference equals (n+1)^d - 2*(n-1)^d, of which the present sequence is the case d = 4. The first n at which this difference becomes negative, as a function of d, is A342831.
%H A400233 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400233/b400233.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400233 a(n) = -n^4 + 12*n^3 - 6*n^2 + 12*n - 1.
%F A400233 a(n) = (n+1)^4 - 2*(n-1)^4.
%e A400233 The cube [0,3]^4 has 256 lattice points in total, of which 16 are interior and 240 are on the boundary, so a(3) = 240 - 16 = 224.
%t A400233 A400233[n_] := n*(n*(n*(12 - n) - 6) + 12) - 1; Array[A400233, 35] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 29 2026 *)
%o A400233 (PARI) a(n) = -n^4 + 12*n^3 - 6*n^2 + 12*n - 1
%o A400233 (Python) def a(n): return -n**4 + 12*n**3 - 6*n**2 + 12*n - 1
%Y A400233 Cf. A342831, A400232, A400235.
%K A400233 sign,easy,new
%O A400233 1,1
%A A400233 _Henrik Arhold_, Sep 21 2026
